{
  "name": "scripts",
  "$schema": "../node_modules/nx/schemas/project-schema.json",
  "sourceRoot": "scripts",
  "projectType": "library",
  "targets": {
    "test": {
      "command": "pnpm run test",
      "options": {
        "cwd": "scripts"
      }
    }
  },
  /* Two suites here spawn `playwright test --list`, which enumerates stories
     from `apps/storybook/storybook-static/index.json` and routes from
     `apps/docs/.next/prerender-manifest.json`. Neither is an import edge Nx
     can see, and until this line `nx show project scripts` reported
     `implicitDependencies: []` — so nx.json's `test: dependsOn ["^build",
     "build"]` resolved to nothing, `scripts:test` entered the graph with
     nothing to wait for, and `nx run-many -t test` scheduled it beside
     `storybook:build`. That target empties `storybook-static` before it
     refills it, so the suite read a directory that was missing, half-written
     or one commit stale depending on when it got there. See the comment at the
     top of visual-builds.mjs for the three shapes that took, and for the
     measurement.
     Deliberately implicitDependencies rather than a per-project `dependsOn`
     on `test`: a per-project `dependsOn` REPLACES the nx.json default rather
     than adding to it (nx.json says so, and #165 settled that ordering
     centrally), so spelling it here would mean re-stating `^build` and
     `build` to keep them. Naming the two projects instead is what makes the
     `^build` already in the default resolve to their builds.
     The cost is that a change to either app now marks `scripts` affected. That
     is correct rather than incidental: the docs routes and the storybook
     stories ARE the shot list these suites assert over. quality.yml already
     forces a whole-repo run for `tests/visual/**` and `playwright.config.ts`
     for the same reason, from the other side. */
  "implicitDependencies": ["storybook", "docs"]
}
